We stood on the threshold stone of the war-nest and read the truth by lantern light.

The nest was never the head of the sickness either. Tunnels run deeper still, down past the drowned galleries, and up from them comes a wind that smells of old fire. The goblins were not conquering when they broke our seal. They were fleeing.

What they fled has followed them up.

It waits now in the Maw — the great broken crater where the Deep Road once met the sky. A troll of the deep dark, grown vast on two hundred years of night, with wyverns wheeling above it like crows above a slaughter. The last of the horde has rallied to it, because even a goblin knows to stand behind the biggest thing in the room.

Thorin checked his axe edge with his thumb and said nothing, which from Thorin is a speech.

A warden's charge is to keep the chronicle. But a chronicle of three dwarves dying alone at the bottom of the world is not one I intend to write. I have set down my pen, taken my mother's axe from the shrine wall, and told Vala to stop grinning about it.

Four of us go to the Maw, then. We end this where our grandmothers ended it. With ENOUGH carved fresh in whatever stone survives.
